Step 7 : Verify that the architecture produced in Iteration 1 satisfies the selected drivers specified in step 2.

- UC1 – Query Info  
    - UC1 is satisfied.  
    - The Presentation Layer captures user messages, the NLU Engine interprets intent, and the Conversation Orchestrator determines which service to contact.  
    - The LMS, Registration, and Calendar Adapters retrieve academic information from external systems and databases.  
    - The Presentation Layer swiftly displays the response back to the user.

- UC3 – Sync External Data  
    - UC3 is satisfied.  
    - The Integration Layer uses adapters that standardize communication with LMS, registration, and calendar systems.  
    - The Adapter/Facade pattern allows the system to integrate with multiple universities despite differences in their APIs.  
    - The Conversation Orchestrator coordinates data synchronization tasks to ensure consistent and reliable updates.

- UC6 – Configure System  
    - UC6 is satisfied.  
    - The Admin Console feature provides tools for administrators to update workflows, policies, and configurations accordingly.  
    - The Configuration Store maintains system settings securely.  
    - Centralized authentication ensures only authorized administrators can make configuration changes. Ensuring security of the application and data integrity.

Verification of Quality Attributes

- QA2 – Scalability  
    - QA2 is satisfied.  
    - Cloud-native deployment enables horizontal scaling across different layers.  
    - The architecture avoids bottlenecks by separating Presentation, Application, Integration, and Data responsibilities.  
    - The adapter pattern isolates external dependencies, preventing performance slowdowns when external APIs are under load. 
    - All of these functions ensure the application is largely scalable.

- QA3 – Interoperability  
    - QA3 is satisfied.  
    - For an application to be interoperable, multiple systems should be allowed to connect, share data, and function together without manual efforts.
    - The Integration Layer standardizes communication with all university systems through adapters.  
    - The Common Integration API ensures the orchestrator interacts with external systems in a unified manner, regardless of API differences.

- QA4 – Availability  
    - QA4 is satisfied.  
    - Cloud infrastructure enables zero-downtime updates and distributed deployment.  
    - The Log & Metrics Service supports rapid detection and recovery from errors.  
    - Separation of components ensures system stability even if one module experiences issues.

Verification of Concerns

- CRN-1 – Security, Access Control, and Compliance  
    - CRN-1  is satisfied.  
    - Centralized SSO authentication provides secure access control.  
    - Sensitive academic and personal data is protected within authenticated components.  
    - Administrative tools enforce proper authorization and compliance with institutional policies.

- CRN-3 – Reliable Integration With External Systems  
    - CRN-3 is satisfied.  
    - Dedicated adapters isolate external API failures and differences.  
    - The Common Integration API provides consistent communication patterns, retry logic, and fallback handling.  
    - These mechanisms ensure stable and synchronized data exchange with university systems.

Verification of Constraints

CON-2 – Cloud-Native, Zero-Downtime Deployment  
    - CON-2 is satisfied.  
    The architecture supports distributed cloud deployment.  
    Independent services can be updated without interrupting system availability.

CON-4 – Ability to Handle 5,000 Concurrent Users  
    - CON-4 is satisfied.  
    Horizontal scaling supports increased concurrent traffic.  
    Decoupled services prevent load on one component from causing slowdowns in others.  
    Asynchronous integration decreases wait times during high-demand periods.